No. No. No.  It has nothing to do with the character name.  It has to
do with the name attached to the account from which you play the game.
 When you first got OS X, you were asked for your full name (what you
see on the log in page if you don't have auto-login set) and a
username (the part after /home/ when you type echo $HOME at a command
prompt).  The username must match the value set in config.h (somewhere
around line 360 or so).  Here is the relevant section of my config.h:

#ifndef WIZARD          /* allow for compile-time or Makefile changes
*/
# ifndef KR1ED
#  define WIZARD  "eric" /* the person allowed to use the -D option */
# else
#  define WIZARD
#  define WIZARD_NAME "eric"
# endif
#endif

The default is wizard, meaning only people who can log into an account
with username wizard can invoke wizard mode.  wizard is not a standard
Unix account, thus a stock compile from source does not allow wizard
mode (unless you're crazy enough to set up a 'wizard' account).  Note
that the second comment says 'person allowed to use the -D option',
not character name.

I can now start up a wizard mode character with any name at all with
the command nethack -uMyName -D, as long as I'm logged in as eric.

